            slug: 'healing-protection',
            title: 'Healing and Protection',
            native: 'נטירותא',
            translit: 'nṭiruta',
            summary: 'What the bowls were actually for: health, sleep, safe childbirth, a sound house and living children, and the writ of divorce served on a demon.',
            paragraphs: [
                'The word that opens a great many of the bowls is asuta, healing. Not gnosis, not ascent, not power over others: health. Read in bulk, the corpus is a record of ordinary trouble in a Mesopotamian village or town. Someone cannot sleep. A woman has lost a succession of children. A man is ill and the illness has not responded. The livestock are dying. A neighbour is thought to have done something. These are not exotic anxieties, and the bowls answer them in the register in which they were felt, which is one reason the corpus resists the older habit of treating magic as the debased end of religion. Nobody commissioned a bowl in order to be interesting.',
                'The scope of protection is spelled out with the thoroughness of a conveyance. A bowl will name the client, then his wife, then his sons and his daughters by name where they are known, then his house and his dwelling, his threshold, his cattle and his possessions, sometimes his fields. The body may be itemised limb by limb. The unborn receive particular attention: a substantial group of bowls is written for pregnant women against the lilith who takes infants, asking that the children of this woman should live and not die. In a world of high infant mortality this is not superstition standing in for medicine but a rational response to a danger for which medicine had little to offer.',
                'Alongside the bowls stand the amulets, qameʿa, and the two share a repertoire. The amulets are thin lamellae of silver, gold, bronze or lead, inscribed in minute script, rolled tightly and carried in a tube at the neck or on the arm; many of the finest come from Palestine and Syria and were edited by Naveh and Shaked. The difference is one of deployment rather than kind: the bowl guards a place and stays there, the amulet guards a body and travels with it. Some bowls call themselves qameʿa outright. Scripture supplies part of the repertoire on both, above all Psalm 91, which rabbinic sources call the song against afflictions, along with the priestly blessing of Numbers 6 and the divine self-designation as healer in Exodus 15:26. The rabbinic discussion at Shevuʿot 15b that names the psalm also records unease at the practice, and draws a distinction — healing oneself by words of Torah is forbidden, guarding oneself is another matter — which is exactly the kind of line that holds in a text and does not hold in a household.',
                'The most arresting device in the corpus is the get, the writ of divorce, served upon a demon. Its logic is legal rather than magical, and that is the point. The lilith is understood to have attached herself to a man as a wife, or to a woman as a husband; the practitioner therefore meets her with the instrument by which a marriage is ended in Jewish law. The bowl follows the shape of the rabbinic formulary, with its dismissal of the spouse and its extinction of her claims, and on that basis requires the demon to depart and not return. Some bowls reach instead for the shamta, the ban, the community\'s other legal weapon, here aimed at a party outside the community altogether. What is remarkable is the jurisprudential move underneath: the practitioner is not asserting a superior force, he is asserting jurisdiction. The demon is treated as a person with standing, capable of being served and therefore capable of being dismissed. The formularies themselves are described here and not reproduced.',
                'None of this displaced the physician. The asya practised alongside the writer of bowls, and the therapeutic pages of the Babylonian Talmud at Giṭṭin 67b to 70b move between pharmacology, dietetics, regimen and adjuration without any sense of crossing a boundary; Markham Geller\'s work on the Akkadian background of those pages shows how much older Mesopotamian medicine is sitting inside them. The boundary is ours. A patient in sixth-century Babylonia might take a remedy, follow a diet, and bury a bowl beneath the threshold, and would have understood all three as prudent measures against the same trouble, differing in mechanism but not in kind.',
            ],
